        "urduTitle": "سوڈیم (Na+) آئنز کا داخل ہونا",
        "text": "Action Potential in a Neuron.\n\nWhen a neuron fiber is stimulated by a stimulus of adequate strength (threshold stimulus), the stimulated area of the fiber becomes several times more permeable to Na* than to the K* due to the opening of voltage regulated Na gates. As a result Na gates permit the influx of Na ions by diffusion. Since there are more Na* ions entering than leaving, the electrical potential of the membrane changes from -70 mV towards zero and then reach to the 50 mV. This reversal of polarity across two sides of membrane is called depolarization. This electropositive inside and electronegative outside lasts for about one milli second till the Na gates are not closed.\n\nAfter the peak of action potential, called the spike potential, the permeability of the membrane to Na' decreases, while it becomes more permeable to K* which rapidly diffuses out from cytoplasm to extracellular fluid due to the opening of K* gates. Now Na* gates are closed. Soon this part of neuron membrane regains its original polarity and becomes electropositive on outside and electronegative on inside. This is called repolarization.",
